# ==========================================================================
# zlib.pc.in -- pkg-config descriptor template for zlib
# ==========================================================================
#
# configure rewrites this template into zlib.pc (the zlib.pc sed block near
# the end of configure), win32/Makefile.gcc performs the equivalent
# substitution at install time, and Makefile.in installs the result into
# $(libdir)/pkgconfig.  The CMake build uses the parallel template
# zlib.pc.cmakein rather than this file.
#
# This descriptor is deliberately implementation-agnostic: the flags below
# describe the C implementation and the memory-safe Rust implementation
# (crates/libz-rs-sys, built as a cdylib and a staticlib) equally well, so
# no Rust-specific variant of this file is needed.  The Rust build
# reproduces the C build's packaging exactly:
#
#   * the artifacts are named libz.so and libz.a, so -lz resolves to either;
#   * the shared object carries SONAME libz.so.1 and ships the same
#     libz.so -> libz.so.1 -> libz.so.1.3.2.1-motley symlink chain, so a
#     program already linked against libz keeps loading it under the name
#     recorded in its DT_NEEDED entry;
#   * the exported symbol surface is identical -- 111 dynamic globals, being
#     95 functions plus the 16 symbol-version nodes declared in zlib.map;
#   * the installed zlib.h and generated zconf.h are byte-identical, because
#     the Rust port modifies neither header.
#
# The public API and ABI are therefore unchanged: a consumer compiled and
# linked against the C library runs against the Rust library without being
# recompiled.  Libs: advertising -lz and Cflags: advertising the include
# directory are part of that guarantee -- do not reword them, and do not add
# Requires: entries, a Libs.private: line, -lm, -lpthread, or any
# Rust-specific flag.  Like the C library, the Rust library links only the C
# runtime, so its consumers need nothing extra.
#
# Two constraints the substitution passes impose on future edits: every
# name=value line must begin in column 0, because configure's rules are
# anchored (/^name *=/s#=.*#=$name#) and would silently skip an indented
# line; and adding a substitutable variable also requires a matching rule in
# configure's zlib.pc sed block -- a different block from the one that
# rewrites Makefile.in -- plus a matching -e in win32/Makefile.gcc.  Prefer
# not to add one.  Never spell a substitution placeholder literally in these
# comments either: both passes substitute globally and would rewrite it here.
# ==========================================================================

prefix=@prefix@
exec_prefix=@exec_prefix@
libdir=@libdir@
sharedlibdir=@sharedlibdir@
includedir=@includedir@

Name: zlib
Description: zlib compression library
Version: @VERSION@
License: Zlib

Requires:
Libs: -L${libdir} -L${sharedlibdir} -lz
Cflags: -I${includedir}
